{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Cost attribution and controls for AI developer and productivity tools on Amazon Bedrock | Artificial Intelligence","context":"## The attribution problem\n\nThe developer and productivity tools run on each user’s machine and call Amazon Bedrock directly, or through an LLM gateway. Users authenticate by assuming a shared IAM role, so every API call appears as `assumed-role/<role>/<session-name>` in billing. If every user assumes the role with the same fixed session name, or calls go through a gateway’s own role, CUR aggregates spend to a single principal. With IAM Identity Center, the session name is the user’s sign-in name, so each user gets their own principal. You can further enrich it with session tags (team, cost center, project). These fields flow into CUR 2.0’s `line_item_iam_principal` column and cost allocation tags with the `iamPrincipal/` prefix, making per-user spend queryable alongside the dollars, with zero changes to your Bedrock API calls.\n\nClaude Platform on AWS and Claude for Enterprise route inference through Anthropic-managed infrastructure. For Claude Platform on AWS, usage is visible by workspace and API key in Anthropic’s admin console, and workspace tags flow to AWS billing. For Claude for Enterprise, per-user usage is visible in Anthropic’s admin console and Usage and Cost API. Spend limits are enforced natively by Anthropic","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/cost-attribution-and-controls-for-ai-developer-and-productivity-tools-on-amazon-bedrock/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Cost","context":"## Option 3: Amazon Bedrock embeddings and LLMs\n\n| AWS service | Dimensions | Cost [$USD] |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Amazon Bedrock for LLM question answering (optional) | Daily average of 8,000 requests each made of 2,000 input tokens and 200 output tokens estimated using Anthropic Claude 3 Haiku (lower cost LLM option) or Anthropic Claude 3 Sonnet (higher cost LLM option) | $180.00 (Haiku) to $2,160.00 (Sonnet) |\n| **Total with Amazon Bedrock embeddings and LLMs ($595.33 + $180.00 to $2,160.00):** | | **$775.33 to $2,755.33** |","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/latest/qnabot-on-aws/cost.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Best practices for cost attribution","context":"## Choose the right mechanism for the question you are answering\n\nEach mechanism is optimized for a different question. Pick based on the answer you need.\n\n* **Per-user or per-team dollars on your bill.** Use IAM principal attribution. The caller identity is captured automatically on every Amazon Bedrock inference call on the `bedrock-runtime` and `bedrock-mantle` endpoints, and you can layer on principal or session tags for team, department, or cost center.\n* **Per-application or per-workload dollars.** Use Application inference profiles on `bedrock-runtime`, or Projects and Workspaces on `bedrock-mantle`. These resources carry cost allocation tags that flow to AWS Cost Explorer and CUR 2.0.\n* **Per-prompt token usage and cost.** Use Per-request metadata tagging with your model invocation logs. Each call is recorded as a separate log entry with its own token counts, and you can slice by any tag you set on the request.\n* **Both invoice-accurate dollars and per-prompt detail.** Combine the two. Many teams run IAM principal attribution for native per-user/team dollars in CUR, and request metadata for the per-prompt token detail in their invocation logs","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/cost-mgmt-best-practices.html"}]}}

